<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Elisu Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1604 CE – 1844 CE (approx.) · capital Ilisu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Tsakhur sultanate in the foothills of north-western Azerbaijan whose last sultan defected from Russia to join Imam Shamil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Elisu Sultanate took shape around 1604 among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsakhurs">Tsakhur</a> people of the southern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, centred on the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilisu">Ilisu</a>. Its rulers, who took the title sultan, emerged as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> reasserted control over the region after wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsakhurs">Tsakhur</a>, Azerbaijani and Georgian-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingiloys">Ingiloy</a> villages, collecting tribute and leading raids into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kakheti</a>. The land lived by herding, orchards and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. Its rulers balanced between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, the khanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezgins">Lezgin</a> free communities of Jar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balakan">Belokan</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The sultanate accepted Russian protection in 1803. In 1844 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniyal-bek">Daniyal Bek</a>, though a Russian major general, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Imam Shamil's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caucasian-imamate">Caucasian Imamate</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished the sultanate. Daniyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bek">Bek</a> became one of Shamil's naibs and later made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/elisu-sultanate.jpg" alt="Elisu Sultanate" width="320" height="167"><figcaption>Image: Seymur06 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Elisu_Sultanate,_Daniyal_Sultan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1604 CE – 1844 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ilisu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Daniyal Bek</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tsakhur; Azerbaijani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ilisu Sultanate, Tsakhur Sultanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1724&amp;focus=elisu-sultanate" aria-label="Elisu Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5828 699 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Elisu Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6148 955L6281 1235M6148 955L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6281 1235h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6148 955h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6148 955h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ilisu. <a href="/atlas?year=1724&amp;focus=elisu-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

